**assistant**:
> Yes. Make each pair a two-choice item, where each choice assigns both statements at once. That enforces one statement in each category.
> 
> ```latex
> \documentclass[addpoints]{exam}
> 
> \newcommand{\classifypair}[2]{%
>   \begin{choices}
>     \CorrectChoice Dynamic vulnerability: #1; \quad
>                    Climate adaptation: #2.
>     \choice        Dynamic vulnerability: #2; \quad
>                    Climate adaptation: #1.
>   \end{choices}
> }
> 
> \begin{document}
> 
> \begin{questions}
> 
> \question For each pair, assign one attribute to \emph{dynamic vulnerability}
> and the other to \emph{climate adaptation}.
> 
> \begin{parts}
> 
> \part
> \classifypair{Driven by income growth}{Driven by warming}
> 
> \part
> \classifypair{Has zero adaptation costs}
>               {Adaptation costs approximately equal adaptation benefits}
> 
> \part
> \classifypair{Flattens the whole dose--response curve}
>               {Flattens the hot-side of the dose--response curve}
> 
> \part
> \classifypair{Limited by an elasticity}
>               {Limited by a maximum level}
> 
> \end{parts}
> 
> \end{questions}
> \end{document}
> ```
> 
> `\CorrectChoice` prints normally for students, but marks the answer when compiling with `\printanswers`. If an item’s correct ordering differs, swap its two macro arguments.
