**assistant**:
> A small **assignment matrix** is clearer: students place one mark in each row *and* each column of each pair.
> 
> ```latex
> \documentclass[addpoints]{exam}
> \usepackage{amssymb} % for \square
> \usepackage{booktabs}
> 
> \newcommand{\boxcell}{$\square$}
> 
> \begin{document}
> 
> \begin{questions}
> \question
> For each pair, place an X in one box in each row and each column.
> This assigns one attribute to dynamic vulnerability and the other to
> climate adaptation.
> 
> \begin{center}
> \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5}
> \begin{tabular}{llcc}
> \toprule
> & & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Attribute} \\
> \cmidrule(lr){3-4}
> Pair & Category & 1 & 2 \\
> \midrule
> 
> 1 & & Driven by income growth & Driven by warming \\
>   & Dynamic vulnerability & \boxcell & \boxcell \\
>   & Climate adaptation    & \boxcell & \boxcell \\
> \midrule
> 
> 2 & & Has zero adaptation costs &
>       Adaptation costs $\approx$ adaptation benefits \\
>   & Dynamic vulnerability & \boxcell & \boxcell \\
>   & Climate adaptation    & \boxcell & \boxcell \\
> \midrule
> 
> 3 & & Flattens the whole dose--response curve &
>       Flattens the hot-side of the dose--response curve \\
>   & Dynamic vulnerability & \boxcell & \boxcell \\
>   & Climate adaptation    & \boxcell & \boxcell \\
> \midrule
> 
> 4 & & Limited by an elasticity & Limited by a maximum level \\
>   & Dynamic vulnerability & \boxcell & \boxcell \\
>   & Climate adaptation    & \boxcell & \boxcell \\
> \bottomrule
> \end{tabular}
> \end{center}
> 
> \end{questions}
> \end{document}
> ```
> 
> This makes the intended constraint visually obvious: every pair gets exactly two marks, arranged diagonally. It also avoids making students parse two long, nearly identical answer sentences.
